{
  "summary": "Replaces raw byte vectors on the script interpreter stack with reference-counted objects so opcodes that duplicate or reorder items copy pointers instead of deep-copying memory. It also simplifies the `EvalScript` interface by replacing an overload wrapper with a defaulted parameter and adds unit tests verifying that stack items share underlying memory.",
  "problem": "The script interpreter deep-copies byte buffers whenever opcodes like `OP_DUP` manipulate elements. While stack elements are presently limited to 520 bytes, future proposed soft forks (such as post-quantum signature schemes) may raise element limits to 10 kB, allowing scripts to cause asymmetric memory inflation and CPU consumption via repeated duplication and hashing.",
  "evidence": [
    "Author description: \"duplicating and hashing cost only a single weight unit in tapscript, with no upper limit on how many times they can be executed... forces all validators to evaluate OP_RIPEMD160 about 4284 times, each on the same 10kb input.\"",
    "Author local benchmark in description: repeated 520-byte element duplication/hashing script takes ~3 ms to validate, whereas a 10 kB element takes ~52 ms."
  ],
  "dependencies": {
    "depends_on": [],
    "enables": [
      "A planned future PR adding a hash cache keyed by interpreter stack element pointers"
    ]
  },
  "scope_notes": "Modifies internal interpreter data types and signatures across `EvalScript` and its callers.",
  "changed_since_previous": "",
  "card": "Changes the script interpreter to store stack elements as reference-counted objects rather than copying byte vectors during duplication opcodes like OP_DUP. This prepares the interpreter for future soft fork proposals that might increase the 520-byte stack limit for post-quantum signatures, while laying groundwork for a pointer-keyed opcode hash cache. The author reports a 10 kB element validation benchmark taking 52 ms versus 3 ms for 520 bytes under repeated hashing, though these claims are solely from the author and address hypothetical post-quantum script limit increases.",
  "needs": [
    "diff"
  ],
  "confidence": "medium",
  "uncertainties": [
    "The exact pointer mechanism and memory management model cannot be verified because the diff is missing."
  ],
  "risk": {
    "level": "high",
    "touches": [
      "consensus",
      "validation"
    ],
    "why": "Any flaw in how stack elements are mutated, shared, or passed within the script interpreter could cause consensus divergence between nodes or memory corruption during block validation."
  }
}
